\title{controlcompR: Control Systems, Optimal Control and Model Predictive Control}
\description{
\if{html}{\figure{logo.svg}{options: style='float: right' alt='logo' width='120'}}

A unified framework for modeling, analyzing, designing and simulating linear and nonlinear control systems. It provides continuous-time and discrete-time state-space and transfer-function models, exact zero-order-hold, first-order-hold and Tustin discretization, time and frequency responses, stability, controllability and observability analysis, Schur-based Lyapunov solvers, Riccati solvers based on ordered generalized Schur decompositions of extended pencils (Van Dooren 1981 \doi{10.1137/0902010}), linear quadratic regulators (LQR), pole placement, proportional-integral-derivative (PID) controllers with derivative filtering, anti-windup and bumpless transfer, Luenberger observers, Kalman filters and smoothers, linear quadratic Gaussian (LQG) controllers, gain, phase and disk margins, H2 and H-infinity norms (Bruinsma and Steinbuch 1990 \doi{10.1016/0167-6911(90)90049-Z}), H2 and experimental H-infinity synthesis, balanced truncation, constrained linear model predictive control (MPC) solved with the operator splitting quadratic program solver 'OSQP' (Stellato et al. 2020 \doi{10.1007/s12532-020-00179-2}), moving horizon estimation, subspace (Van Overschee and De Moor 1994 \doi{10.1016/0005-1098(94)90230-5}) and autoregressive with exogenous input (ARX) system identification, and nonlinear simulation, linearization, extended and unscented Kalman filters, trajectory optimization and nonlinear model predictive control. The design is inspired by 'python-control' \url{https://python-control.readthedocs.io/} and 'ControlSystems.jl' \url{https://github.com/JuliaControl/ControlSystems.jl}; neither 'Python' nor 'Julia' is required.
}
